<p>A <strong>denied insurance claim in Port Arthur</strong> often arrives weeks after a storm, when you have already started cleaning up and assumed help was on the way. Some denials are correct: the policy excludes the cause, or the damage really is older than the storm. Others are built on a short inspection, a misread cause of loss or missing information. The difference matters, and the way to find it is to compare the denial letter, the policy and the physical evidence side by side. We help homeowners and businesses in Port Arthur, Groves, Nederland, Sabine Pass and throughout Jefferson County do exactly that.</p>

<h2>Why Claims Get Denied on the Upper Texas Coast</h2>
<p>Port Arthur sits in one of the most storm-exposed corners of Texas. Hurricanes Rita, Ike, Laura and Delta, and the rainfall from Harvey and Imelda, all left their mark on the region. Several features of the local insurance landscape make denials more likely here than in many inland areas:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Split policies.</strong> Many homes have a homeowners policy that excludes wind, a separate TWIA wind policy and a separate flood policy. Each insurer may attribute the damage to a peril the other one covers.</li>
<li><strong>Wind versus water.</strong> Hurricanes bring wind, wind-driven rain, storm surge and rainfall flooding at once. Standard policies exclude flood and surge, so the cause of each area of damage is often the whole dispute.</li>
<li><strong>Catastrophe volume.</strong> After a major storm, insurers bring in large numbers of outside adjusters, and inspections can be brief.</li>
<li><strong>Older roofs and homes.</strong> Wear-and-tear arguments are common where roofs and houses are older, even when storm damage is present.</li>
<li><strong>High percentage deductibles.</strong> A hurricane deductible of 2% or 5% of Coverage A can make an incomplete estimate look like it falls below the deductible.</li>
</ul>
<p>For a deeper look at the wind-and-water question, read <a href="/wind-vs-flood-damage-port-arthur-insurance/">wind vs. flood damage in Port Arthur</a>.</p>

