<h2>Denials Involving TWIA and Split Policies</h2>
<p>When a home has both a homeowners policy and a TWIA windstorm policy, a denial from one insurer is not the end of the analysis. If the homeowners carrier denies because the damage came from wind, the TWIA claim should be filed and pursued. If TWIA attributes interior damage to something other than wind, such as a long-term roof leak, the homeowners policy may or may not respond depending on its terms. And if surge or rising water caused part of the loss, only a flood policy will cover that portion. TWIA claims also have their own filing deadlines and dispute procedures under Texas Insurance Code Chapter 2210. Our <a href="/services/twia-windstorm-claims/">TWIA windstorm claims</a> page covers those in more detail.</p>

<h2>What to Do After Receiving a Denial</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Get the reason in writing.</strong> If the letter is vague, ask the insurer to identify the specific policy language and facts it relied on.</li>
<li><strong>Request the reports.</strong> Ask, in writing, for copies of any engineer, inspector or adjuster report and the photos used to make the decision.</li>
<li><strong>Get the full policy.</strong> Request a certified copy, including all endorsements, for the policy period of the loss.</li>
<li><strong>Preserve evidence.</strong> Keep damaged materials, photos and video. Continue protecting the property from further damage.</li>
<li><strong>Check the weather record.</strong> Our <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup</a> and <a href="/storm-history/">storm history</a> pages can help connect damage to a date.</li>
<li><strong>Track deadlines.</strong> Write down every deadline in the letter and the policy.</li>
</ol>

